 "findings": {
  "L3_PERIOD": "Salesforce (FY ends Jan 31): the statement header is ONE month-day over three years ('Fiscal Year Ended January 31,' / '2023 2022 2021'); fy_end amendment 2 pairs month-days with years only when counts match (the Walmart guard), so the columns keep the filer's labels 2023/2022/2021 and every FY/FY-1 cell is a year off. The manifest test (first year == manifest FY + 1) already separates Salesforce (2023 vs 2022) from Walmart (2022 vs 2022), so broadcasting a lone month-day is gated by the same rule. L4 also labels the filer's fiscal 2022 as FY2022 (value 26,492).",
  "L3_LABEL": "Netflix/Boeing: L3 text-row extracts the FY-1 value with the right period but the caption is not in TEXTROW_SYNONYMS ('net cash used in operating activities', 'net cash used provided by operating activities', 'net loss earnings', 'property plant and equipment additions'), so it is stored under a slug and never scores.",
  "L4_REPLY": "Secondary on the same 4 cells: every saved typed reply for these fields carries the current year only (FY2019); the L4 stage never supplies FY-1, so FY-1 depends entirely on L3.",
  "CANONICAL": "BOEING net_income 2019: raw HIT (-636), canonical picks -677 from the equity-statement row 'Net earnings/(loss) (636) (41) (677)' (column total, L4 reply on page 57).",
  "unseen_vs_seen": "raw 36/48 (0.75) here vs 71/76 (0.93) on AGG-1.13 docs; 8 of the 12 misses are one filing's period labels, 4 are caption-vocabulary gaps on FY-1; with both fixed the pilot would read 48/48 raw (counterfactual, misses only)."
 },
